Text: "Linear algebra studies addition and scalar multiplication when both are
       compatible with a field of scalars. Vector spaces provide the objects,
       linear maps provide the morphisms, and bases translate the abstract
       theory into coordinates and matrices. Dimension records the finite
       amount of independent information in a space; duality turns vectors
       into linear measurements; and eigenvalue theory identifies directions
       on which an operator acts by pure scaling.

       Coordinates are a tool, not part of the underlying space. Statements
       formulated invariantly survive a change of basis, while matrix entries
       generally do not. The chapter repeatedly moves between these two levels
       so that computation remains attached to its structural meaning."
